  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 2f4848e7e46289243b311920bdbdddf0, type: 3}
  m_Name: Pathfinding
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  playerLines:
  - Navigation systems are wild
  - How do they know how to get from one place to another?
  - Neat. So that's how my games work
  - <i>hmm...</i>
  - <i>So pathfinding can move certain objects into empty spaces</i>
  - <i>Some objects can even push other objects along</i>
  NPCLines:
  - Pathfinding is an integral study for a variety of fields ranging from graph theory
    and AI to engineering
  - One of the simplest and most intuitive methods of pathfinding was introduced
    by Edsger W. Dijkstra in 1956
  - Dijkstra's algorithm outlines a simple method to iteratively find the shortest
    path between 2 points relatively quickly
  - A* is a more effective varient of Dijkstra's algorithm which is more widely used
    in a variety of applications such as video games and car navigation
  - It takes a heuristic approach to pathfinding, considering the distance to the
    destination as well the start
  - Pathfinding algorithms can be used to take anything from one point to another
  - Depending on the object, they may even push other objects out of the way to reach
    their goal
  speakTurnPlayer: 01010000000000010000010101
